course: biology
lesson: 77
title: Endangered animals and extinction
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Extinct means gone for ever: when the last animal of a kind dies, no zoo, scientist or wish can bring that kind back.
explain: Look at Martha, the stuffed passenger pigeon in her glass case: her kind once flew in flocks of billions that darkened the sky for hours, yet she died alone in a zoo in 1914, so even the commonest animal can disappear.
explain: Most extinctions today are caused by people, by cutting down homes, hunting, bringing in rats and diseases, and dropping plastic; the good news is that the same people can protect animals, and tigers in India and Nepal are already increasing.
explain: Cartoons draw the dodo as a fat, stupid bird that deserved to vanish; in truth it was well suited to its island, and it died out because sailors' rats, pigs and monkeys ate its eggs.
fact: The dodo was a big flightless pigeon from Mauritius, last reliably seen in 1662, less than 100 years after sailors arrived; with no enemies it was not afraid, so sailors hunted it easily, and animals brought by ships ate its eggs.
fact: Passenger pigeons lived in flocks of billions in North America, but hunting and the loss of forests wiped them out, and Martha, the last one, died at Cincinnati Zoo in 1914.
fact: The thylacine, or Tasmanian tiger, was a striped, dog-like pouched mammal; farmers blamed it for killing sheep and were paid to kill it, and the last known one died in Hobart Zoo in 1936, and despite many reported sightings there has never been clear proof any survived.
fact: Wild tigers live only in Asia; there may have been about 100,000 a century ago and only a few thousand now, but numbers in India and Nepal have grown in recent years thanks to protection.
fact: Orangutans live only on Borneo and Sumatra and are critically endangered, as their forest is cut for timber and palm oil; a mother stays with her baby for about 7 to 8 years.
fact: Sea turtles, especially leatherbacks that eat jellyfish, can mistake floating plastic bags for food; plastic can block the stomach so the turtle feels full and starves.
fact: The vaquita, the world's smallest porpoise at about 1.5 metres, lives only in the far north of the Gulf of California; it drowns in illegal nets, and recent surveys counted only about 10 left.
fact: Rhinos are hunted illegally for horns that some people wrongly believe are medicine, though rhino horn is keratin like hair and fingernails; only two northern white rhinos are left, both female, Najin and Fatu.
fact: Many bees have become fewer because of lost wild flowers, some pesticides, disease and changing weather, which matters because about three quarters of the main food crops benefit from pollinators; there are around 20,000 kinds of bee, and most live alone in little holes, not in hives.
fact: A fungus disease called chytrid, spread partly by the animal trade, has harmed hundreds of kinds of frogs by attacking the skin they breathe and drink through; the Panamanian golden frog now survives only in breeding centres.
fact: The IUCN Red List records how endangered each species is, from least concern through vulnerable, endangered and critically endangered to extinct, and more than 47,000 species on it are judged threatened.
fact: The great auk was a large flightless seabird of the North Atlantic, first called a penguin, though it was no relative of the penguins of the far south; it was easy to catch on land, and the last known pair was killed in Iceland in 1844.
exercise: Where did the dodo live? | On the island of Mauritius | In the forests of North America | On the ice of Antarctica | In the jungles of Borneo
exercise: What kind of bird was the dodo? | A big flightless pigeon | A small flying parrot | A giant penguin | A kind of ostrich
exercise: What was the main reason the dodo died out? | Animals from ships ate its eggs, and people hunted it | It was far too stupid to find enough food on its own island | It was too fat to walk | A volcano covered its island
exercise: Who was Martha? | The last passenger pigeon, who died at Cincinnati Zoo in 1914 | The very last dodo, kept in a cage on a Dutch ship until it died in 1700 | The first panda born in a zoo | A scientist who counted pigeons
exercise: What was the thylacine? | A striped, dog-like pouched mammal from Tasmania | A real tiger, just like the Indian ones, that once lived in Australia | A large flightless bird | A small striped kangaroo that hopped
exercise: Why were farmers paid to kill thylacines? | They were blamed for killing sheep | Their striped fur was used to make warm coats | They ate the farmers' crops | They carried a disease to cows
exercise: Where do wild tigers live? | Only in Asia | In Africa and Asia | In South America | All over the world
exercise: Why are orangutans critically endangered? | Their forests are cut for timber and palm oil | They are hunted and eaten by the tigers that live in Africa | They cannot find enough bananas | Cold winters kill their babies
exercise: Why do sea turtles swallow plastic bags? | Floating bags look like the jellyfish they eat | They use the bags to build soft nests for their eggs on the beach | They like the taste of plastic | They are trying to clean the sea
exercise: Where does the vaquita live? | Only in the far north of the Gulf of California | In every ocean of the world, from the poles to the tropics | In the rivers of the Amazon | Under the Arctic ice
exercise: What is a rhino horn made of? | Keratin, like hair and fingernails | Ivory, just like an elephant's tusk | Bone, like a deer's antler | Stone, like a fossil
exercise: Which of these harms bees? | Loss of wild flowers, some pesticides and disease | Too many flowers growing close together in gardens | Rain on summer evenings | Children watching them in gardens
exercise: How do most kinds of bee live? | Alone, in little holes, not in hives | In huge hives with a queen | Under water near ponds | In the nests of birds
exercise: Why is the chytrid fungus so dangerous to frogs? | It attacks the skin they breathe and drink through | It eats up all the insects that frogs need to catch for food | It makes ponds too hot | It blocks frogs' ears so they cannot hear
exercise: Where does the Panamanian golden frog survive now? | Only in breeding centres | Wild in every single river and stream of Panama | Only in Australian deserts | In pet shops around the world
exercise: What is the IUCN Red List? | A world record of how endangered each species is | A list of the most dangerous animals in the world to people | A list of animals that are red | A shopping list for zoos
exercise: What was the great auk? | A large flightless seabird, the first bird called a penguin | A giant eagle from Iceland with feathers as long as an arm | A huge seal with feathers | A small flying puffin
test: In a cartoon, the dodo is a fat, silly bird that waddles off a cliff. What is right? | The dodo suited its island and died out because of people and ship animals | Yes, the dodo was too stupid to live, which is why it fell off cliffs and died | Yes, all dodos fell off cliffs | The dodo is not extinct and lives in zoos
test: «there were billions of them, no way a bird like that dies out» What does the passenger pigeon show? | Even the commonest animal can disappear when hunted and its forests lost | Common animals can never die out, however many people hunt them | Only rare animals ever go extinct | Passenger pigeons are still flying today
test: «my uncle saw a Tasmanian tiger last year, swear down» What is right? | Many sightings were reported, but no clear proof any survived | Yes, thousands still live in the forests of Tasmania today | Yes, the zoo released some in 2000 | No, thylacines never existed at all
test: «rhino horn cures fevers, my mate's gran says» What is right? | Rhino horn is keratin like fingernails and has no healing power | Yes, rhino horn cures every illness | Yes, but only if it is ground into powder and mixed with hot water | No, rhino horn is poisonous to eat
test: What would happen if a vaquita and a tiger both lost the same number of animals? | The vaquita, with only about 10 left, would be closer to extinction | The tiger would be in more danger, as it is bigger and needs more food | Both would be equally safe | Neither, because both live everywhere
test: Why does a long childhood make orangutans slow to recover? | Each mother raises a baby for about 7 to 8 years, so few are born | Long childhoods make orangutans weak and lazy when they grow up | Babies never leave their mothers | Orangutans stop having babies after one
test: «it's only one plastic bag, the sea's massive, who cares» What is right? | A turtle can mistake it for a jellyfish, and plastic can make it starve | Plastic dissolves in the sea within a day | Turtles never eat anything that floats on top of the water, only things on the sea bed | One bag can't reach the sea from a beach
test: «no bees, so what, I don't even like honey» Why would losing bees matter? | Many food crops benefit from pollinating animals like bees | Bees only make honey and do nothing else that is useful to anybody | Bees are pests that eat fruit | Losing bees would make more flowers grow
test: «penguins live up north too, the great auk was one» What is right? | The great auk was first called a penguin but was no kin of southern penguins | Yes, penguins live at both poles today | No, the great auk was a kind of eagle that lived on the cliffs of Iceland | No, the great auk was a seal
test: Which Red List category would a very common bird like a pigeon be in? | Least concern | Critically endangered | Extinct | Endangered
test: Why are animals on islands often in more danger than those on big continents? | They live in a small area and often have no defence against new animals | Islands are always too hot | Island animals are slower thinkers than animals on the big continents and cannot learn | Islands sink into the sea every year
exam: Identify the principal cause of the extinction of the dodo upon the island of Mauritius. | The predation of its eggs by animals introduced by ships, and hunting | Its own lack of intelligence, which prevented it from finding sufficient food | An eruption of a volcano which covered the island | A severe winter which froze the island
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the composition of rhinoceros horn. | It consists of keratin, the material of hair and nails, without medicinal property | It consists of ivory and possesses proven healing properties | It consists of bone and cures fevers when it has been ground to a powder and dissolved in water | It consists of stone and is poisonous when ingested
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the IUCN Red List? | It is a worldwide record of the degree to which each species is threatened | It is a register of the animals most dangerous to human beings | It is a catalogue of animals of a red colouration, compiled for the purposes of identification | It is a schedule of animals available for purchase by zoos
exam: State the year in which the last known passenger pigeon, named Martha, died at Cincinnati Zoo. | Nineteen hundred and fourteen | Sixteen hundred and sixty-two | Nineteen hundred and thirty-six | Eighteen hundred and forty-four
